{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>Dweller of the Negev.</b> This is Amalek, as it is written, ״עמלק יושב בארץ הנגב.״ And if it is Amalek, why is he called a Canaanite? Rather, when HaKadosh Baruch Hu saw that he came back and fought with Israel a second time, HaKadosh Baruch Hu said: he is not forbidden to you like the children of Ammon, about whom I said to you, ״אל תתגרו בם,״ but rather he is to you like the Canaanites, about whom it is said, ״כי החרם תחרימם,״ and therefore he is called a Canaanite. Here Rashi explained: Israel saw their garments were like the garments of Amalekites — but he did not cite it according to Rabbi Eliezer ha-Kalir, who said in the passage of Zachor: ״כסות ולשון שנה היות מלך ערד״ (garment and changed tongue — that the king of Arad was). One may say that this verse is among the verses missing a word, and so the reading is: ״וישמע הכנעני מלך ערד ועמלק ישב בארץ הנגב״ etc. And we find in another place that both of them were in one settlement and in one agreement, as it is written: ״והעמלקי והכנעני יושב בעמק,״ ״כי העמלק והכנעני שם [בעמק] [לפניכם],״ ״וירד העמלקי והכנעני.״" "<b>דרך האתרים (way of the Atharim).</b> Inverted letters, like ״כבשים כשבים,״ ״שמלה שלמה״ — so too here, האתרים is like התארים, meaning going around, like ״ותאר הגבול.״ When he heard that Israel was coming by way of the circuits of the land of Edom, from north to south, as it is written, ״ויסעו מהר ההר דרך ים סוף לסבוב את ארץ אדום,״ he thought they were coming upon him, for he was in the south of Edom, as it is written, ״יושב הנגב״ — therefore, ״וילחם בישראל.״"], :book :numbers, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:18:59.862821984Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}